{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And Yitzchak said to them.</b> One needs to know how Yitzchak could answer the king and his officers with ״מַדּוּעַ בָּאתֶם אֵלַי״ — for this is not proper conduct. Further, his actions in the end proved the opposite, for he made a covenant with them. Further, after he said ״וְאַתֶּם שְׂנֵאתֶם אוֹתִי״, what place is there to elaborate and say ״וַתְּשַׁלְּחוּנִי״ and so on? And if he is bringing proof of the hatred by saying ״וַתְּשַׁלְּחוּנִי״, he should have said ״כִּי שִׁלַּחְתֶּם אוֹתִי״ and so on." "However, Yitzchak's intention relates to what he perceived: that there is no circumstance in which Avimelekh would need Yitzchak for anything, except out of fear for his inheritance — lest they lay hand on it by virtue of the master's gift. And this matter — Avraham had already sworn concerning it, as we raised the difficulty in its place: why did he go to him? And Yitzchak's heart was whole not to violate the covenant that he had made with Avraham his father, for the halakhah follows the Rambam that whoever vows upon the understanding of his fellow requires release in his presence — meaning, with his knowledge. And since Avraham did not release the oath before Avimelekh, [it remains] כִּרְאִי מוּצָק (Job 37:18), so that the sons can no longer release it. From this point on, they have no need of him, and in this respect he said ״מַדּוּעַ בָּאתֶם״ — surely this coming is only for Yitzchak's own needs, to set his mind at ease and to restore him to the place. Therefore he said the word <b>אֵלַי</b> — meaning, even if the coming is for my needs, I judge even that as far-fetched to believe, for ״אַתֶּם״ and so on. He was precise in saying <b>וְאַתֶּם</b> to say: besides all the people of the city, even the king and his officers hated me — and this hatred is not contingent on any thing, but rather because you were jealous of me and could not tolerate letting me remain with you in the city; this is what he means by <b>וַתְּשַׁלְּחוּנִי</b> and so on. And this is hatred born of jealousy. Now it is well known that any hatred contingent on some thing — when the thing ceases, [the hatred ceases] and so on; but hatred born of jealousy never ceases, unless the thing through which the jealousy arose is nullified. From this point on, your minds will not change to remove your hatred from me."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:43.349160790Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}